	•	Section 5 Comments/Quotes: The composite adverse outcome comprised receipt of simultaneous heart-kidney transplantation or, after heart transplantation alone, chronic dialysis, eGFR below 20 ml/min per 1.73 m², or kidney-transplant listing/transplantation within 1 year. In the first temporal external-validation cohort of 1,567 recipients, the random forest achieved c-statistic 0.899, sensitivity 0.680, specificity 0.941, PPV 0.680, NPV 0.941, and F1-score 0.680. A 95% confidence interval for the c-statistic and model accuracy were not reported. Calibration and DCA were not reported. The second postpolicy validation cohort achieved a c-statistic of 0.874.

Section 6: Quality Assessment (Risk of Bias / PROBAST)
	•	Participants (Selection Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The intended population was heart-transplant candidates at waitlisting, but model development included only candidates who ultimately underwent heart transplantation or simultaneous heart-kidney transplantation. Patients with missing creatinine or dialysis status were excluded, and nontransplanted candidates were not represented, creating selection and verification bias for the intended waitlist use.
	•	Predictors (Input Variable Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: Most predictors were measured at listing, but eGFR and dialysis status were also obtained immediately before transplantation, approximately 35–40 days later. These later measurements are not available at the initial waitlisting decision and introduce temporal mismatch when the model is described as a tool “at the time of heart transplant listing.”
	•	Outcome (Definition Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The composite outcome combined an actual clinical allocation decision—receipt of SHKT—with objective posttransplant kidney outcomes. SHKT receipt depends on center practice, clinician judgment, and policy rather than confirming irreversible kidney disease, creating incorporation and treatment-decision bias.
	•	Analysis (Modeling Bias): High Risk | Quote/Reasoning: The large cohort, hyperparameter search, five-fold cross-validation, and two temporal external validations were strengths. However, calibration was not assessed, confidence intervals were not reported, and it was not clearly stated whether KNN imputation and feature selection were fitted separately within each cross-validation fold. The model and decision threshold were selected through an extensive search, increasing optimism risk.
	•	Applicability to Review Question: Low Concern | Quote/Reasoning: The study directly evaluates a machine-learning model intended to guide allocation decisions in heart-transplant candidates and predicts clinically relevant posttransplant kidney outcomes. It is directly applicable to AI research in thoracic organ transplantation.

